40 | woodlots | Woods and rocks on the edge of the Slaughter Pen above the Plum Run gorge | - | 1863-07-02 | stated | The study says the 4th Maine was exposed to a frontal and enfilading fire from the Plum Run gorge and from the woods and rocks on the edge of the Slaughter Pen | =Unfortunately, the 4th Maine was unable to hold out by itself in this site, {{e@|}} exposed to a frontal and enfilading fire from Plum Run gorge and from the woods and rocks on the edge of the Slaughter Pen, and had retreated to the comparative safety of the rest of the brigade atop the Den. ~O
43 | woodlots | A point of woods, the northern finger of Rose Woods, bordered the eastern edge of the Wheatfield | bld-rose-farm | 1863-07-02 (the typescript prints Wehatfield) | stated | The study says the 40th New York moved through the point of woods, or northern finger of Rose Woods, bordering the eastern edge of the Wheatfield (the typescript prints Wehatfield) | =Moving {{throught|through}} the point of woods, or northern finger of Rose {{Hoods|Woods}} bordering the eastern edge of the Wehatfield, the 40th New York took their first position at "the short piece of stone wall in front of the two guns of Smith's 4th New York Battery. ~O
43 | fences | A short piece of stone wall stood in front of the two guns of Smith's battery on the hillock | - | 1863-07-02 (the phrase quoted from the 40th New York's report as the study gives it) | stated | The study quotes the 40th New York's report that its first position was at the short piece of stone wall in front of the two guns of Smith's battery | =Moving {{throught|through}} the point of woods, or northern finger of Rose {{Hoods|Woods}} bordering the eastern edge of the Wehatfield, the 40th New York took their first position at "the short piece of stone wall in front of the two guns of Smith's 4th New York Battery. ~O
43 | fields | A meadow sloped down from the two guns of Smith's battery to the rocky ravine below | - | 1863-07-02 (the phrase is quoted from Bachelder's notes of about 1875; hedged) | hedged | The study quotes Bachelder's notes that the 40th New York charged down the meadow to the rocky ravine below | =The regiment responded by firing a few volleys into the enemy towards Devil's Den, and charging "down the meadow to the rocky ravine below." ~O | =[John B. Bachelder. (Notes on Services of Troops at the Battle of Gettysburg) (c. 1875).
43 | general | The 40th New York moved down the bed of Plum Run, many men up to their knees in mud and water | - | 1863-07-02 (the phrase is quoted from the regiment's official report as the study gives it) | stated | The study says the 40th New York moved down Plum Run itself, straddling the creek bed at the double-quick, many of the men up to their knees in mud and water | =Their advance was particularly memorable to the Mozart Regiment since they moved down Plum Run {{jtself,|itself,}} straddling the creek bed at a double-quick, many of the men "up to their knees in mud and water. ~O
46 | fields | A meadow or flat lay in the rear of the Georgians' position on Houck's Ridge | - | 1863-07-02 (evening; Robertson's letter of 1882 as the study quotes it; hedged) | hedged | The study quotes Robertson's letter that artillery was ploughing up the ground in the meadow or flat immediately in the rear of his position on Houck's Ridge during the evening | =since later throughout the rest of the evening {{“seyeral|"several}} pieces of artillery was playing on and literally ploughing up the ground in the meadow or flat immediately in the rear of [his] position. ~O | =[Robertson to Bachelder, 11 May 1882.]
47 | earthworks | Stone wall breastworks thrown up in the woods at the base of Big Round Top on the night of July 2 and early July 3 by most of Robertson's and Law's forces | - | 1863-07-02 night to 1863-07-03 early morning (built on those hours, not before) | stated | The study says that on the night of July 2 and early the next morning most of Robertson's and Law's forces moved to the south and east, throwing up stone wall breastworks in the woods at the base of Big Round Top | =That night, and early in the morning of the following day, most of Robertson's and Law's forces moved to the south and east, throwing up stone wall breastworks in the woods at the base of Big Round Top. ~O | =The fighting in the area of the Slaughter Pen and Weikert's Field, and at this southern crest of Houck's Ridge, ended with nightfall of 2 July.
47 | general | The Confederate dead of the Slaughter Pen and Weikert field lay among the boulders and bushes until Union burial details came; the photographs were made before | - | 1863-07 (after the Confederate retreat began; the photographs were recorded before the burial details) | stated | The study says the Union burial details (the typescript cuts the word burial at the margin and prints buria) interred the Alabama, Georgia and Texas dead who were scattered about the boulders and bushes, and that the photographs of the dead at this end of the field were recorded before the details reached these areas | =It would be the lot of Union buria details to inter the dead from Alabama, Georgia, and Texas who were scattered about the boulders and bushes. The photographs of the dead at this end of field were recorded before these details reached these areas, and provide a striking testimony to the significance of battle action in this area. ~O
48 | woodlots | In 1984 the vegetation of the Slaughter Pen, the Plum Run gorge and the Sherfy woodlot was over-extended and obscured the ground | bld-sherfy-farm | ~1984 (the vegetation of 1984; the study gives no 1863 condition here) | stated | The study says the most pressing need is clearing out the over-extended vegetation in the Slaughter Pen, the Plum Run gorge and the Sherfy woodlot, which obscures the movements and activities of the battle | =Overpowering all others is the responsibility of clearing out the over-extended vegetation in the {{Staughter|Slaughter}} Pen, the Plum Run gorge, and the Sherfy Woodlot. The movements and activities in this, one of the most historic sites on the field, are obscured by vegetation to such an extent that it is not only impossible to interpret what happened there, but it is ignoring the story of the men who fought and died there. ~O
48 | general | Smith's guns stood in front of the infantry line at the crest of Houck's Ridge, perhaps 50 yards ahead | - | 1863-07-02 (the study's conclusion from the recorded history; 'perhaps 50 yards') | hedged | The study says Smith's guns were in front of the infantry line at the crest of Houck's Ridge by some distance, perhaps 50 yards | =Smith's guns were in front of the infantry line at the crest of Houck's Ridge, by some distance (perhaps 50 yards). ~O
48 | later | The section of Smith's battery that stood on the hillock at the north end of Houck's Ridge in 1863 now stands in the ravine on Crawford Avenue, where the War Department put it in 1900 | - | !1900 (the guns as the War Department placed them; the study puts the 1863 position on the hillock at the northern end of Houck's Ridge) | stated | The study says the section to the rear is on Crawford Avenue in the Valley of Death instead of on Houck's Ridge as in 1863, that the Bachelder position maps of 1863 and the 1880s and the trolley court-case map show its guns on the hillock at the northern end of Houck's Ridge, and that the War Department put them in the ravine in 1900 | =The section to the rear is presently on Crawford Avenue, in the Valley of Death, instead of on Houck's Ridge as it was in 1863. The Bachelder troop position maps (1863 and draft of 1880s) accurately portrayed the position of these guns on the hillock at the northern end of Houck's Ridge, as did the map prepared for the court case against the trolley. The fact that the 40th New York stopped {{fn|in}} front of the section and then charged "DOWN the meadow" to the "rocky ravine BELOW" indicates that the guns were on a height and not within the "rocky ravine" itself as they have been since put there by the War Department in 1900. ~O
52 | general | Plate 1: The Spring at Devil's Den (Tipton #2471); the den itself is beneath the top boulder and behind the spring | - | ~after 1863 (a Tipton view; the typescript gives no date) | stated | The caption of plate 1 says the view shows the spring at Devil's Den (Tipton number 2471) and that the den itself is beneath the top boulder and behind the spring | =1. The Spring at Devil's Den (Tipton #2471)---the den {{itse}f|itself}} is beneath the top boulder and behind the spring. ~O
53 | later | Plate 2: The Trough Rock at Devil's Den (Tipton #2466) shown in use at about the turn of the century | - | ~turn of the century (a Tipton view of a horse and rider at the trough; part of the caption is covered by a mark on the page) | stated | The caption of plate 2 reads The Trough Rock at Devil's Den (Tipton number 2466) shown in use, and its second line reads turn of the century; the words between are covered by a mark on the page | =The Trough Rock at {{Devit's|Devil's}} Den (Tipton #2466)---shown in use ~O
54 | general | Plate 3: O'Sullivan's photograph of the north end of the Slaughter Pen and the Plum Run valley, 6 July 1863, with the trough boulder encircled | - | 1863-07-06 (the date the study gives) | stated | The caption of plate 3 says the photograph by O'Sullivan of 6 July 1863 shows the north end of the Slaughter Pen and the Plum Run valley and that the large boulder with the trough visible in it is encircled | =3. North end of Slaughter Pen and Plum Run Valley (O'Sullivan), 6 July 1863-- the large boulder with the trough visible in it is encircled ~O
55 | general | Plate 4: Gardner #229, 6 July 1863, a dead Confederate in the rocks and boulders of the Plum Run gorge | - | 1863-07-06 (the date the study gives; the unit is the study's 'most likely') | hedged | The caption of plate 4 says the stereo view Gardner number 229 of 6 July 1863 most likely shows an infantryman of the 2nd Georgia, since it was obviously taken in the mass of rocks and boulders of the Plum Run gorge | =4. Dead Confederate soldier (Gardner #229), 6 July 1863---this stereo view most likely portrays one of the infantrymen of the 2nd Georgia Infantry, since the photograph was obviously taken in the mass of rocks and boulders of Plum Run gorge, which was the route of attack of that regiment of Benning's Brigade. ~O
56 | general | Plate 5: O'Sullivan's view in the Slaughter Pen at the foot of Big Round Top, 6 July 1863, looking east from Devil's Den | - | 1863-07-06 (the date the study gives) | stated | The caption of plate 5 says the view by O'Sullivan of 6 July 1863 in the Slaughter Pen at the foot of Big Round Top is eastward from the Devil's Den | =5. View in the Slaughter Pen at the foot of Big Round Top (O'Sullivan), 6 July 1863---the view is eastward from the Devil's Den ~O
57 | general | Plate 6: Gardner #265, 6 July 1863, two Confederate dead in the Slaughter Pen with the trolley road bed to the right of the picture | - | 1863-07-06 (the photograph; the trolley road bed the caption names is a later feature, not part of the 1863 scene) | hedged | The caption of plate 6 says the view Gardner number 265 of 6 July 1863 shows two dead infantrymen in the Slaughter Pen, probably of the 44th Alabama, and that the trolley road bed is just to the right of the picture | =6. Confederate dead in the Slaughter Pen (Gardner #265), 6 July 1863--- this view shows two dead infantrymen, probably of the 44th Alabama Infantry, which suffered casualties from the rifles of the 4th Maine {{gand|and}} from Smith's two guns on the hillock of Houck's Ridge. The trolley road bed is just to the right of the picture. ~O
58 | woodlots | Plate 7: Gibson #252, 6 July 1863, the woods at the northwestern base of Big Round Top at the edge of the clearing around Plum Run | - | 1863-07-06 (the photograph; the place is the study's reading, 'probably') | hedged | The caption of plate 7 says Gibson number 252 of 6 July 1863 shows a scene in the woods at the northwestern base of Big Round Top, probably just at the edge of the woods near the clearing around Plum Run, above the present comfort station | =7. Scene in the woods at the northwestern base of Big Round Top (Gibson #252), 6 July 1863---also identified as "in Slaughter Pen, foot of Round Top", this photograph probably depicts more of the dead of the 44th Alabama killed by the first volley from the 4th Maine, just at the edge of the woods near the clearing around Plum Run. The area would be just above the present comfort station. ~O
59 | general | Plate 8: Gardner #277, 6 July 1863, a dead Confederate near the edge of the G. W. Weikert field | - | 1863-07-06 (the date the study gives; the unit is the study's 'most likely') | hedged | The caption of plate 8 says the view Gardner number 277 of 6 July 1863 shows a victim of Ward's brigade near the edge of the G. W. Weikert field, most likely of the 20th Georgia or the 1st Texas | =8. Dead Confederate soldier, Devil's Den (Gardner #277), 6 July 1863--- this view portrays a victim of Ward's Brigade near the edge of the G.W. Weikert field, and is most likely a member of the 20th Georgia {{weer oy|or}} the {{ist|1st}} Texas killed in the attack on Smith's Battery. ~O
